# Response headers for the landing page.
#
# Written from what the deployment was actually sending. Before this file every
# response carried only Cloudflare's defaults, which meant `Cache-Control: public,
# max-age=0, must-revalidate` on the fonts as much as on the HTML: every visit
# revalidated all three font files before any text could draw. There were no
# security headers at all.
#
# The syntax is a URL pattern on its own line, then the headers to attach to
# anything matching it, indented beneath. A request matching several patterns
# inherits all of them.

# The only rule that changes behaviour. These files are stable, self-hosted, and
# replaced by replacing the file, so a year of not asking again is right. The names
# carry no content hash, which is the one thing that would justify longer.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Two images that change when the design does. A week keeps them out of the way
# without making a redesign take a year to show up.
/og.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/apple-touch-icon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# Everything else. None of these is sent today, and none of them can break a static
# page with no forms and no third-party scripts.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

# Deliberately absent:
#
# Strict-Transport-Security. workers.dev is on the HSTS preload list, which was
# checked rather than assumed (hstspreload.org answers "preloaded" for it), so
# browsers already refuse plain HTTP for this host and the header would change
# nothing at all. It becomes worth adding the day this moves to its own domain.
#
# Content-Security-Policy. Every script on the page is inline and so is the whole
# stylesheet, which is the page's stated design: one file, no build step, no
# render-blocking request. A policy would therefore have to allow
# `script-src 'unsafe-inline'` and would buy close to nothing. A real CSP means
# moving the script and the stylesheet into files of their own, reversing that
# decision. If that ever happens, the policy needs api.github.com in `connect-src`,
# because the download button reads the release list from it.
#
# Cache-Control on the HTML. Cloudflare's default of `max-age=0, must-revalidate`
# is exactly right for a page whose content changes on every deploy.
